ITEM 16 -- RC100 (z = 0.6-2.5) through the full kernel, not the deep-MOND formula
====================================================================================================================
  RC100: 100 galaxies with z, M_bar, R_e, V_c(R_e); z = 0.61 - 2.52; 10 flagged deep-MOND (g < a_0)
  kernel inversion succeeds for 41/100 (the rest have g_obs <= g_bar, i.e. no boost to invert)
  sample                                 N   median a_0                 16-84%   median y = g_bar/a_0
  all with a kernel inversion           41    1.433e-10    3.90e-11 - 2.65e-10                   1.50
  low acceleration (y < 2)              28    1.796e-10    3.75e-11 - 2.66e-10                   1.05
  table's own deep-MOND flag             8    8.192e-11    2.81e-11 - 1.77e-10                   0.58
  the repo's ledger uses a_0 = V_c^4/(G M_bar): median 1.854e-10 on the same deep subset
  [all] d log a_0/dz = +0.0666 +- 0.0858 (N = 41); framework FLAT requires 0.000 (+0.8 sigma), LambdaCDM-native requires +0.1314 (-0.8 sigma)
  [y < 2] d log a_0/dz = +0.0233 +- 0.1348 (N = 28); framework FLAT requires 0.000 (+0.2 sigma), LambdaCDM-native requires +0.1314 (-0.8 sigma)
  [PASS] 16 RC100 through the framework's own kernel is CONSISTENT WITH the flat law and cannot yet exclude the LambdaCDM-native rise: d log a_0/dz = +0.023 +- 0.135 on the low-acceleration subset, 0.2 sigma from flat and 0.8 sigma from the rising alternative.  Reported as underpowered, not as a discriminator   (d log a_0/dz = +0.0233 +- 0.1348 (N = 28); flat at +0.2 sigma, LambdaCDM-native (+0.131) at -0.8 sigma; separating them needs the error below 0.045, i.e. ~9x this sample or a factor 3 better per galaxy)
  [PASS] 16b AGAINST INTEREST, and worth its own line: 58 of 100 RC100 galaxies have g_obs <= g_bar at R_e -- the framework predicts MORE acceleration than is observed for most of the sample, so no a_0 can be inverted for them at all.  That is the high-redshift 'baryon-dominated, falling rotation curve' result (Genzel+2017; Nestor-Shachar+2023 low dark-matter fractions) seen from the framework's side, and it is a liability the flat-a_0 law has to answer   (58/100 = 58% with g_obs <= g_bar; a pressure-support correction (sigma_0 is tabulated, median 46 km/s) moves V_c up and would recover some, and is the first thing to try)
  caveats, both ways: V_c is measured at R_e rather than on the flat part, so a_0 = f(V_c^4) scatters hard; the sample is
  mass-selected and its median acceleration is above a_0, which is where a fitted a_0 is least reliable; and the gas masses come
  from scaling relations.  This is a constraint on the TREND, which those systematics largely cancel in, not on the level.

